  <notes-header>

  <chapter*|A constructive introduction to quantum field theory>

  <notes-abstract|Lectures at SISSA, Trieste, 20 October--9 November 2026,
  as part of the Probability and Stochastic Analysis Trimester Programme.>

  <with|font-series|bold|Lecturer.> Massimiliano Gubinelli (University of
  Oxford).

  <with|font-series|bold|Joint work.> The results presented in Lectures
  4--7 are joint work with Sarah-Jean Meyer, obtained recently with the use
  of AI; those of Lectures 5--7 are new.

  <with|font-series|bold|Acknowledgements.> M. Gubinelli is partly
  supported by UK Research and Innovation via the grant ``StochFields''
  EP/Z534328/1 and by the Simons Collaboration on Probabilistic Paths to
  QFT. S.-J. Meyer is supported by the grant ``StochFields'' EP/Z534328/1.

  This course is part of the <hlink|Probability and Stochastic Analysis
  Trimester Programme|https://sites.google.com/view/probsissa> at SISSA.

  <subsection*|Abstract>

  In these lectures I will review some mathematical problems associated to
  the definition and construction of quantum field theories. Mainly from
  the probabilistic viewpoint but also paying some attention to the quantum
  aspects. The goal is to give a wide perspective on the open problems and
  opportunities for research, in the era of large language models.

  <subsection*|Lecture materials>

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 1> (20 October 2026): Relativistic
    quantum fields and imaginary time. Wightman axioms and quantum fields
    as operator-valued distributions; the two-point function; positivity
    of energy and continuation to imaginary time; the Feynman--Kac
    representation, lattice spin systems and the Osterwalder--Schrader
    axioms.
    Notes: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_lecture_1.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_lecture_1.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_lecture_1.tm>].
    Slides: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_slides_1.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_slides_1.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_slides_1.tm>].
    Revised 7 November 2026.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 2> (22 October 2026): The Gaussian
    free field. Construction on the torus and Euclidean space; reflection
    positivity and the free relativistic field; Nelson's Markov property
    and Symanzik's Brownian-path representation; Wick products; Gaussian
    integration by parts and the Dyson--Schwinger equation.
    Notes: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_lecture_2.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_lecture_2.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_lecture_2.tm>].
    Slides: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_slides_2.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_slides_2.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_slides_2.tm>].
    Revised 7 November 2026.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 3> (30 October 2026): Stochastic
    quantisation and stochastic observables. Parabolic and elliptic
    stochastic quantisation of the Gaussian free field; decomposition of the
    field by resolution; stochastic observables, with examples from Brownian
    motion and the free field; Wick powers in every dimension, products of
    observables and the operator product expansion.
    Notes: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_lecture_3.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_lecture_3.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_lecture_3.tm>].
    Slides: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_slides_3.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_slides_3.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_slides_3.tm>].
    Revised 7 November 2026.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 4> (4 November 2026): Forward--backward equations. Renormalisation, optimal control and filtering as three ways to generate the interacting measure; force coordinates; the value function as a generator, the Legendre transform and the Wetterich equation.
    Notes: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_lecture_4.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_lecture_4.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_lecture_4.tm>].
    Slides: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_slides_4.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_slides_4.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_slides_4.tm>].
    Revised 7 November 2026.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 5> (5 November 2026): Observables and field equations. Observables of the interacting theory; from the Minkowski to the Euclidean field equation, and Nelson's Euclidean noise; the field equation at every resolution, with a sub-Gaussian noise; Dyson--Schwinger equations, also in pathwise form; infinite volume.
    Notes: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_lecture_5.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_lecture_5.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_lecture_5.tm>].
    Slides: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_slides_5.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_slides_5.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_slides_5.tm>].
    Revised 7 November 2026.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 6> (6 November 2026): The FBSDE in force coordinates, two renormalisations and the large-field problem.
    Notes: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_lecture_6.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_lecture_6.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_lecture_6.tm>].
    Slides: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_slides_6.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_slides_6.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_slides_6.tm>].
    Revised 7 November 2026.
  </itemize>

  <subsection*|Provisional materials for Lecture 7>

  Lectures 6--7 give an FBSDE construction of
  <math|\<phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on the torus. The materials for Lecture 7
  may be adjusted as the course progresses. Sections 16--18 of Lecture 7 will not
  be delivered in class.

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 7> (9 November 2026): A priori estimates, UV stability and the limiting FBSDE: an explicit density for the measure and a renormalised variational formula (Sections 16--18 not delivered in class).
    Notes: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_lecture_7.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_lecture_7.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_lecture_7.tm>].
    Slides: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_slides_7.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_slides_7.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_slides_7.tm>].

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Supplement to Lectures 6--7>: Detailed proofs of what the lectures cite or condense: the coefficient construction, the large-field ledger, consistency of the coefficients, and older routes.
    Notes: [<notes-store|PDF|sissa/2026/cqft_supplement.pdf>]
    [<notes-link|HTML|sissa-2026/cqft_supplement.tm>]
    [<notes-store|TeXmacs|sissa/2026/texmacs/cqft_supplement.tm>].
  </itemize>

  Materials revised 7 November 2026. The TeXmacs sources of all notes
  and slides, together with the style packages they use, are also
  available as one archive [<notes-store|zip|sissa/2026/cqft_texmacs_sources.zip>]. In the HTML slides, use the arrow
  keys or click to navigate; press <verbatim|o> for an overview.

